    "body": "<p>Elephants communicate partly in infrasound, sound below about 20 hertz, the lower limit of typical human hearing. Their rumbles, produced in the larynx and felt as much as heard, carry through air and also couple into the ground as seismic waves that other elephants detect through their feet and trunk tips. Infrasound&#x27;s long wavelength lets it travel kilometres across savanna with relatively little loss, so separated family groups can stay in contact, coordinate movement, and warn of danger over distances where higher sounds would fade. Researchers record these rumbles with specialised low-frequency microphones and seismometers, and playback experiments have shown elephants responding to rumbles played back from kilometres away. The elephant voice is therefore a reminder that a soundscape contains far more than humans can hear unaided.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: spectrograms of elephant rumbles show strong energy below 20 hertz, and seismic recordings confirm ground-coupled components travelling through soil. Playback experiments in which recorded rumbles were played to wild elephants produced approach, bunching, and vocal replies consistent with the rumbles&#x27; social meanings, demonstrating that the signals are received and acted on. Anatomical studies link rumble production to the larynx and to the large nasal passages that shape the sound.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: at 15 hertz in air, wavelength = 343 / 15 = 22.87 metres, about 22.9 metres, far longer than a human is tall, which is why infrasound bends around trees and terrain. The demo prints 22.87 m at 15 Hz, 34.30 m at 10 Hz, and 17.15 m at 20 Hz, each computed as 343 / frequency.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published elephant communication literature (Poole, Payne, and colleagues on infrasonic rumbles; seismic communication studies); figures as published October 2026</p>",
